\section{The quantile rule}{

The estimate at \code{p} is the smallest sampled value of \code{y} whose estimated
distribution function is \code{p} or more, which inverts the step function
\code{F} without interpolating between sampled values. It is always a value
some sampled row has. This is the rule of
\code{survey::svyquantile(qrule = "math")}, survey's default, and with equal
weights it is \code{stats::quantile(type = 1)}. \code{p = 0} gives the smallest
sampled value and \code{p = 1} the largest.
}

\section{Interval and standard error}{

The interval is Woodruff's (1952). At the estimate \code{q}, \code{F(q)} is a Hajek
mean of the indicator \code{y <= q}, and its variance is the variance of the
total of the linearised residuals \code{((y <= q) - F(q)) / N_hat}, with
\code{N_hat = sum(w)}, from the design's own estimator, as for \code{\link[=ht_mean]{ht_mean()}}.
"Variance" and "Confidence intervals" in \code{\link[=ht_total]{ht_total()}} describe the
estimators, the fallback to the jackknife, the degrees of freedom and the
notes. The interval \code{F(q)} plus or minus \code{t} standard errors, with \code{t} on
the design's degrees of freedom, is carried back through the quantile
rule, so its ends are the estimated quantiles at its two ends. The standard
error is the interval's width divided by \verb{2 t}, and \code{variance} is its
square. \code{cdf} and \code{cdf_se} in the result hold \code{F(q)} and its standard
error.

Where the interval for \code{F(q)} runs below 0 or above 1, the sample bounds
the quantile on one side only. That end of \code{ci} is then \code{NA}, and so are
\code{se} and \code{variance}, with a note.

At the largest sampled value \code{F(q)} is 1 and its residuals are 0, so its
estimated variance is 0 whatever the spread of \code{y}, and the interval
would have no width. A quantile there, which a \code{p} close to 1 or a \code{y}
with one value gives, gets no standard error, with a note, unless every
frame row the design can reach was taken with certainty, when it is
exact. A quantile whose sampled rows lie in one primary sampling unit
gets none either, as a Hajek mean does, because its residuals sum to
zero over that unit.

A quantile has no design effect, and \code{deff} is \code{NA}.
}

\section{Domains}{

\code{by} gives the quantiles of each domain, each from the distribution
function of the domain's sampled rows. The variance of \code{F(q)} is that of
the domain's Hajek mean of \code{y <= q}, from the whole sample, as described
under "Domains" in \code{\link[=ht_total]{ht_total()}}, and the degrees of freedom are the
design's. The result is a \code{drawn_by} table with a row for each domain
and probability.
}

\section{Comparison with survey}{

For the designs \code{\link[=as_svydesign]{as_svydesign()}} maps with identical standard errors,
the estimate, the interval and the standard error equal those of
\code{survey::svyquantile(ci = TRUE, interval.type = "mean")} on its result,
whose interval is also centred on \code{F(q)} rather than on \code{p}. They differ
in these places:
\itemize{
\item At the largest sampled value survey gives an interval of no width and a
standard error of 0, where \code{ht_quantile()} gives none.
\item Where the interval for \code{F(q)} passes 0 or 1, survey gives \code{NaN} for
that end and the standard error, where \code{ht_quantile()} gives \code{NA}.
\item For a \code{p}, or an end of the interval for \code{F(q)}, below the share of the
weight held by the smallest sampled value, survey's rule gives the
second smallest value and \code{ht_quantile()} the smallest.
\item With \code{by}, \code{survey::svyby()} with \code{survey::svyquantile()} forms each
interval on the degrees of freedom of the domain's own units, and
\code{ht_quantile()} on the design's, as for every domain estimate here.
With \code{df = Inf} in both, the standard errors agree.
}

For systematic, spread and systematic PPS samples the variance of \code{F(q)}
comes from the estimator this package uses for their totals, which
\code{survey} does not have.

A missing \code{y} is refused. \code{survey::svyquantile(na.rm = TRUE)} on
\code{\link[=as_svydesign]{as_svydesign()}} estimates the quantiles over the rows that have one.
}

